/// #2065 wall 2: an `Async` entry whose body spawns a suspend-class task.
///
/// The injected entry boundary used to be refused beside
/// `TaskGroup::spawn_suspend`, as "mixing the step convention for one effect".
/// That mismatch was only a spelling. The boundary arm was tail-resumptive
/// because it wrote `resume(v)` in callee position. When the program spawns
/// suspend-class tasks, the arm now binds `resume` first, which makes the same
/// handler suspend-class (linked_compile.vibe, `lc_inject_async_sleep_boundary`),
/// and it goes inside the entry's exception boundary so that the suspend
/// lowering sees no nested handle.
///
/// What remained was the body handed to `TaskGroup::run`: it sits under a row
/// variable. That body is Async-free at the call site because
/// `TaskGroup::spawn_suspend` has a CONCRETE row without `Async`, so it
/// discharges whatever its task performs. `scps_inert_taint` now reads it that
/// way, including after the task literal has been lifted to a top-level name
/// or its step clone.
///
/// 21 + 21 = 42. The value can only come from the spawned body running to
/// completion through the in-guest scheduler while the entry's boundary
/// settles the `sleep`.
///
/// Host futures and streams under such an entry stay refused
/// (`err_async_boundary_mixed_operand.vibe`,
/// `err_async_boundary_host_waitable_spawn.vibe`): a spawned task cannot yet
/// park on a host waitable (ADR-0089 D2's third park kind).
import @vibe/concurrent {
  TaskGroup, TaskHandle
}

fn main() -> Int allows Async + Exception {
  sleep(1)
  TaskGroup::run((g) -> {
    let h = TaskGroup::spawn_suspend(g, () -> Int with Async + Exception {
      let _ = perform Async::Suspend(0)
      21
    })
    TaskGroup::pump_all(g)
    let v = TaskHandle::join(h)
    v + 21
  })
}
